⟨-1,0,1⟩" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406826 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406826 1084 #uuid "652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406827 1081 "⟨-2,0,1⟩" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406827 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406827 1084 #uuid "e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406823 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406824 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406825 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406826 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406827 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1077 "selection" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406824 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406828 1080 #uuid "3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406828 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1069 "q-71231" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1072 #uuid "320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406829 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406830 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406832 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406830 1062 #uuid "1cff8fcf-d3d9-54eb-b0e4-27eb94529c2d" 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406830 1063 #edb/ref 17592186338924 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406831 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406830 13194139533521 17592186045585 true] [17592186406831 1062 #uuid "1dbe7c48-28df-580f-b8a4-86e8677a0881" 131941395335